  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showery and stormy pattern continues through\nSaturday night. \n\n\nEarly evening update reflects less coverage of showers/storms\nper radar trends and mesoanalysis reflects better convergence\nacross the lower Ohio Valley. A front and upper forcing may\narrive toward Friday morning giving the WV mountains some shower\nchances, but looks like tonight is going to be dry for most.\n\nPrevious discussion...\n\nBermuda high pressure continues to weaken over the Mid Atlantic \nregion today and through the weekend. This opens the door for a \nmore active pattern of weather including numerous chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms as mid level troughing dips into the \narea from the north. A cold front was situated over the OH \nValley and continued to shift slowly south. A swath of mid level\nenergy was helping to trigger numerous showers across KY, WV, \nand parts of northern VA. \n\nSome of this activity will reach the mountains of SW VA this\nafternoon and into the overnight hours, however the better \nforcing for ascent still remains to our north for the afternoon.\nCAMs show repeated rounds of rain develop and move east ahead \nof and along the front which will essentially stall near or just\nnorth of the WV/VA border overnight. Expecting most of the \nheavier rainfall to be just north of our forecast area, but \nrepeated moderate rainfall may occur over the Greenbrier Valley \nand perhaps part of the southern Shenandoah Valley along the \nfront where moisture convergence will be maximized. These are \nthe most likely spots for any flooding issues through the \novernight hours. Overall the severe threat looks low and we \nremain in a Marginal Risk per SPC, with damaging winds the main \nconcern with any stronger cells. \n\nThe front will continue to slowly move south through the\nforecast area tomorrow before stalling in the Carolinas. \nExpect heavier and more concentrated activity near and along the\nBlue Ridge with the front in the area tomorrow afternoon and \nevening. We should see higher rainfall rates and an increased \nchance for training storms possibly leading to urban and flash \nflooding. With so much cloud cover, only weaker storms are \nexpected. \n\nA large batch of rain is in store for Saturday afternoon and\nevening areawide, as a southern stream short wave approaches \nand lifts the front north. Most of this activity should shift \neast of the area by midnight. Again we will mainly be concerned \nwith a flooding threat, especially where we receive higher \nrainfall amounts tonight and tomorrow. \n\nSunday through Tuesday look quieter with mostly dry weather, and \nthis quiet stretch may even last longer than that. We will see a\nsimilar set up with a slow-moving, strung out front to our \nnorth for mid week into the end of next week.",
